#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0eb038c-5b51-5d7c-bdf5-f61c5f197752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447870} {:answer/id #uuid "971fa534-d27a-5df8-a351-be678116035b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "971fa534-d27a-5df8-a351-be678116035b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447871} :answer-field/id #uuid "40fcbc27-f09e-5d13-b63b-a3bfac864c91" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448975} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ba547b1-a183-561a-904b-acf399df45c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447872} {:answer/id #uuid "3477959e-ae28-590c-8534-0de29bece5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3477959e-ae28-590c-8534-0de29bece5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447873} :answer-field/id #uuid "d27d2a30-f6b5-5889-b3e8-3a1ab661f5a8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448976} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d226a48-bc9a-5c4f-b777-3375deb35e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447874} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd9c72e-2a23-5a4f-a198-740e3160f17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd9c72e-2a23-5a4f-a198-740e3160f17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447875} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab34820-b3b2-5e7d-b74b-405abb292421" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3976bf87-6856-55b9-92ed-31a90b18feea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447876} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f9c511-78a7-51fe-b700-8504529e7988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3976bf87-6856-55b9-92ed-31a90b18feea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447876} :answer-field/id #uuid "66584c33-4333-5200-8df1-e753f58cf7e7" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3b977ec-88a0-5b97-96bf-4f197129ba2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107119" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $T$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is closed under matrix addition\n- **II.** $T$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **III.** $T$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **IV.** $T$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ are two matrices from $T,$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nis also an element of $T.$ So, $T$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}\\in T$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ 0 & \\alpha c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nalso belongs to $T.$ So, $T$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $T$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd0fc7c-99d8-5050-a35b-ea1c9e5595da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{y}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531962} {:answer/id #uuid "16b1ed05-f11e-5af1-bc34-24beec772bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy-{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531978} {:answer/id #uuid "36ad00a7-4313-5719-9082-bbc20b1b1a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{y}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532044} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f11af5-f6e7-587e-ad2a-37a69de5389a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(2y-{e}^{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532354} {:answer/id #uuid "db6bfd8c-6f2d-50b7-aeb8-7a436d51fb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{y}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3f11af5-f6e7-587e-ad2a-37a69de5389a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(2y-{e}^{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532354} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8115568-baaa-5b24-a293-0a1071a31bac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a8ebfaa-e511-5ff1-bf18-6d98ce82aec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126531" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=x{e}^{y}\\,i+y{x}^{2}\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy-x{e}^{y} \\\\  & =x(2y-{e}^{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b7e6da-6d30-525d-8a9c-47ef27ba45a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531947} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea643b2-c41f-5096-b6fe-334e6989a960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532065} {:answer/id #uuid "67a19838-7bcb-545f-a361-0a1b98dc302f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532165} {:answer/id #uuid "72f6dd0d-4a10-5c1d-a421-4b4ae1d9b96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532191} {:answer/id #uuid "fb30483f-0a87-5374-933a-bc1a646375fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb30483f-0a87-5374-933a-bc1a646375fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532481} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a13513b-95d4-51c9-8314-ccefb6f59b40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f119c93-93f6-598e-a447-4e444111dcb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133264" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+5y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-5y$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+2y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2x-3y$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+5y)=5, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-5y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+2y)=2, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-3y)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c6b39b-576f-5d97-9b83-652a48db42d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269302} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf0af89-e021-5490-a819-9f756ef3896f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269499} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef8b2ce-113f-59c1-b52c-50c667a9e201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "diagonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269982} {:answer/id #uuid "a491fe99-f37d-5931-9e0c-92c000892a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270737} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7abc93-06f6-501b-ab2b-8f9702f1269c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03c6b39b-576f-5d97-9b83-652a48db42d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269302} :answer-field/id #uuid "0729283d-52e9-5aea-9cb2-fad2853b7b13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "828b3c9d-913c-55f0-bf08-423c9d84e9d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-141344" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is }\\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WWW}}\\text{ }\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is \"invertible\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd368cc-9a2b-551e-b564-ee313f513e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517541} {:answer/id #uuid "20deeaf4-0740-55aa-9dcb-2e6f140632ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517545} {:answer/id #uuid "79c50765-9d84-524e-b588-8c069de3e84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517717} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d57042-0b74-581c-8b12-7c36887caccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517895} {:answer/id #uuid "fb517908-0db1-516e-8b98-2030ddb3dcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d57042-0b74-581c-8b12-7c36887caccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517895} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff11dfc-8ed3-50df-b798-7c071f983a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fd4a0bf-2844-5191-8ab1-3fd5afa7e748" :question/math-academy-id "q-343666" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+18y=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=18\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "913ad9eb-89eb-5a60-ac19-fc37d153f23a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439933} {:answer/id #uuid "38454e86-b9b2-5147-b5f5-57a1c469fc1